  user_input {
    user_response: "MedsAlarm Android App Prompt\nTarget: Native Android app (Kotlin/Java), not a web app. Use Android\342\200\231s AlarmManager/WorkManager and Notification APIs so scheduled alarms fire even if the app is closed. Follow Material You (Material 3) design with Android 13+ theming and colors.\n\nSplash Screen\nSimple full-screen splash: app logo centered, title \342\200\234MedsAlarm\342\200\235 and subtitle \342\200\234Medication Reminders Made Simple\342\200\235.\nDisplay for ~3 seconds before navigating to the home screen.\nHome Screen UI\nTop App Bar: A Material 3 TopAppBar (pinned) with your app icon on the left, then the app title and subtitle. Next to that (one line), display the current date/time as Today is <Day>, <MMMM dd, yyyy> | hh:mm:ss AM/PM. Use a color scheme from Material You (e.g. containerColor = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primaryContainer, titleContentColor = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primary). Ensure the bar\342\200\231s text is compact (ellipsize as needed).\nFloating Action Button: A prominent \342\200\234+\342\200\235 FAB at bottom-right (anchored, not part of a bottom bar). Use an accent color from the Material 3 color scheme (e.g. MaterialTheme.colorScheme.secondary).\nAlarm List: Below the app bar, a scrollable list of today\342\200\231s active medication reminders. Each item shows:\nMedicine Name (bold or large text).\nFrequency Text (e.g. \342\200\2341 Tab 3x a Day\342\200\235) below the name in smaller font.\nStatus Text below that (e.g. \342\200\2342 Done, 1 Pending\342\200\235).\nOn the right side of the item, a \342\200\234Take\342\200\235 button. Clicking \342\200\234Take\342\200\235 marks one dose as done (update status counts and background color as described below).\nDisplay a countdown timer (HH:MM:SS) for the next scheduled dose in each item (e.g. \342\200\234Next in 04:15:30\342\200\235).\nDynamic styling: If a medication\342\200\231s all doses for the day are completed (e.g. 3 of 3 done), change that list item\342\200\231s background to light green. Remove the \342\200\234Take\342\200\235 button and instead show a green \342\200\234Done\342\200\235 label. Tomorrow the status resets (show it undone with full counts again).\nThe list should be compact so multiple items fit on screen (no duplicates for the same medicine at multiple times). Scrolling the list should not move the top app bar or FAB (i.e., use a fixed AppBar and FAB with a nested scroll for the content).\nAdd-Alarm (\342\200\234+\342\200\235) Screen\nWhen the user taps \342\200\234+\342\200\235, navigate to a form:\n\nText fields:\nMedicine Name (text).\nDosage (e.g. with hint \342\200\2343 mg\342\200\235).\nInstruction (optional, multiline text).\nMedication Form Icons: Radio buttons or a segmented control with icons (tablet, capsule, injection, etc.).\nTime Picker: A time spinner (AM/PM) similar to Android\342\200\231s native time picker, to select the first dose time.\nRepeat Days: Seven toggle buttons (S M T W T F S). Toggled on = include that weekday for repetition.\nDose Count: A numeric control (e.g. dropdown or spinner) for \342\200\234Dose times per day\342\200\235 (e.g. 1x, 2x, 3x, 5x). Once selected, automatically schedule subsequent doses that day by evenly spacing 24h (for example, 2x \342\206\222 +12h for second dose, 3x \342\206\222 +8h intervals, etc.).\nNotification Options: Three checkboxes: \342\200\234Status Bar Notification\342\200\235, \342\200\234Sound Alarm\342\200\235, \342\200\234Vibrate Alarm\342\200\235. All are enabled by default. These correspond to the app posting a notification with appropriate defaults (sound/vibrate) at each alarm. (Use NotificationCompat with Notification.DEFAULT_ALL or equivalent to include sound/vibration.)\nSet Button: Saves the reminder and schedules the alarms. After saving, go back to Home and show the new item(s).\nAlarm Scheduling & Notifications\nUse Android\342\200\231s AlarmManager (or setExactAndAllowWhileIdle) to schedule alarms at the specified times (for the current day and repeating on selected weekdays). Each alarm should fire a PendingIntent to a BroadcastReceiver (even if the app is not running, per Android docs: \342\200\234Alarms operate outside of your application, triggering even if the device is asleep\342\200\235). In the receiver, create and post a notification using NotificationManager. The notification must have:\n\nA valid channel (create one in onStartup; Android 8.0+ requires a NotificationChannel).\nSound and/or vibration if those checkboxes were enabled (use NotificationCompat.Builder and setDefaults(Notification.DEFAULT_ALL) for both by default).\nThe notification taps should either open the app or simply dismiss.\nAs the ProAndroidDev guide notes, exact alarms require the special permission and a NotificationChannel.\nPermissions & Manifest\nIn AndroidManifest.xml, declare the needed permissions:\n\nxml\nCopy\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.USE_EXACT_ALARM\" />    <!-- if targeting API 33+ -->\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS\"/>\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.VIBRATE\"/>\nSCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM/USE_EXACT_ALARM: Required on Android 12+ to allow exact-time alarms.\nPOST_NOTIFICATIONS: Required on Android 13+ for runtime notification permission.\nVIBRATE: Allows using the device vibrator (normal permission).\nAlso declare the BroadcastReceiver that will handle alarms. If you want reminders to persist after reboot, include RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED and re-schedule alarms on boot.\n\nMaterial 3 Styling and Theming\nUse Material Design 3 (Material You) components and color scheme. For example, use MaterialTheme.colorScheme for theming. In Compose, a TopAppBar could use:\n\nkotlin\nCopy\nTopAppBar(\n  colors = TopAppBarDefaults.topAppBarColors(\n    containerColor = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primaryContainer,\n    titleContentColor = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primary\n  ),\n  title = { /* \342\200\246 */ }\n)\nThis ensures your UI follows Android 12/13 dynamic color guidelines.\n\nImplementation Notes\nFor each reminder, schedule one or more alarms via AlarmManager.setExactAndAllowWhileIdle() or setAlarmClock(), so it fires at precise times even in Doze mode. Android docs emphasize using AlarmManager for exact, timely work (WorkManager is for deferrable work).\nHandle user time zone or locale changes as needed (re-schedule if the user changes clock).\nUpdate the list in real-time: when a dose time is reached (either via the alarm or if the app is open), decrement Pending, increment Done, and update the countdown timers.\nIf all doses done, change item background and replace \342\200\234Take\342\200\235 button with \342\200\234Done.\342\200\235 By default, the item background should be a light color (e.g. surfaceColor), turning light-green when completed.\nPutting it all together, your Google AI Studio prompt should specify the above UI elements, behaviors, and permissions. Emphasize a single-activity architecture with one main screen (AppBar + list + FAB) and one add-reminder screen, using Android\342\200\231s native UI components and services. Embed references to AlarmManager and Notification usage for reliable reminders.\n\nSample Manifest Snippet: <manifest ...>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM\"/>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.USE_EXACT_ALARM\"/>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS\"/>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.VIBRATE\"/>\n  <application ...>\n    <!-- Activity and receiver declarations -->\n    ...\n  </application>\n</manifest>\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Build an Android app\"\nicon: \"android_color\"\n---\n\n"
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Enable high thinking\"\nicon: \"network_intelligence\"\n---\n\nYou MUST add thinking mode to the app where relevant to handle users\' most complex queries. You MUST use the gemini-4-argon model and set `thinkingLevel` to `ThinkingLevel.HIGH`. Do not set `maxOutputTokens`."
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      text: "MedsAlarm Android App Prompt\nTarget: Native Android app (Kotlin/Java), not a web app. Use Android\342\200\231s AlarmManager/WorkManager and Notification APIs so scheduled alarms fire even if the app is closed. Follow Material You (Material 3) design with Android 13+ theming and colors.\n\nSplash Screen\nSimple full-screen splash: app logo centered, title \342\200\234MedsAlarm\342\200\235 and subtitle \342\200\234Medication Reminders Made Simple\342\200\235.\nDisplay for ~3 seconds before navigating to the home screen.\nHome Screen UI\nTop App Bar: A Material 3 TopAppBar (pinned) with your app icon on the left, then the app title and subtitle. Next to that (one line), display the current date/time as Today is <Day>, <MMMM dd, yyyy> | hh:mm:ss AM/PM. Use a color scheme from Material You (e.g. containerColor = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primaryContainer, titleContentColor = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primary). Ensure the bar\342\200\231s text is compact (ellipsize as needed).\nFloating Action Button: A prominent \342\200\234+\342\200\235 FAB at bottom-right (anchored, not part of a bottom bar). Use an accent color from the Material 3 color scheme (e.g. MaterialTheme.colorScheme.secondary).\nAlarm List: Below the app bar, a scrollable list of today\342\200\231s active medication reminders. Each item shows:\nMedicine Name (bold or large text).\nFrequency Text (e.g. \342\200\2341 Tab 3x a Day\342\200\235) below the name in smaller font.\nStatus Text below that (e.g. \342\200\2342 Done, 1 Pending\342\200\235).\nOn the right side of the item, a \342\200\234Take\342\200\235 button. Clicking \342\200\234Take\342\200\235 marks one dose as done (update status counts and background color as described below).\nDisplay a countdown timer (HH:MM:SS) for the next scheduled dose in each item (e.g. \342\200\234Next in 04:15:30\342\200\235).\nDynamic styling: If a medication\342\200\231s all doses for the day are completed (e.g. 3 of 3 done), change that list item\342\200\231s background to light green. Remove the \342\200\234Take\342\200\235 button and instead show a green \342\200\234Done\342\200\235 label. Tomorrow the status resets (show it undone with full counts again).\nThe list should be compact so multiple items fit on screen (no duplicates for the same medicine at multiple times). Scrolling the list should not move the top app bar or FAB (i.e., use a fixed AppBar and FAB with a nested scroll for the content).\nAdd-Alarm (\342\200\234+\342\200\235) Screen\nWhen the user taps \342\200\234+\342\200\235, navigate to a form:\n\nText fields:\nMedicine Name (text).\nDosage (e.g. with hint \342\200\2343 mg\342\200\235).\nInstruction (optional, multiline text).\nMedication Form Icons: Radio buttons or a segmented control with icons (tablet, capsule, injection, etc.).\nTime Picker: A time spinner (AM/PM) similar to Android\342\200\231s native time picker, to select the first dose time.\nRepeat Days: Seven toggle buttons (S M T W T F S). Toggled on = include that weekday for repetition.\nDose Count: A numeric control (e.g. dropdown or spinner) for \342\200\234Dose times per day\342\200\235 (e.g. 1x, 2x, 3x, 5x). Once selected, automatically schedule subsequent doses that day by evenly spacing 24h (for example, 2x \342\206\222 +12h for second dose, 3x \342\206\222 +8h intervals, etc.).\nNotification Options: Three checkboxes: \342\200\234Status Bar Notification\342\200\235, \342\200\234Sound Alarm\342\200\235, \342\200\234Vibrate Alarm\342\200\235. All are enabled by default. These correspond to the app posting a notification with appropriate defaults (sound/vibrate) at each alarm. (Use NotificationCompat with Notification.DEFAULT_ALL or equivalent to include sound/vibration.)\nSet Button: Saves the reminder and schedules the alarms. After saving, go back to Home and show the new item(s).\nAlarm Scheduling & Notifications\nUse Android\342\200\231s AlarmManager (or setExactAndAllowWhileIdle) to schedule alarms at the specified times (for the current day and repeating on selected weekdays). Each alarm should fire a PendingIntent to a BroadcastReceiver (even if the app is not running, per Android docs: \342\200\234Alarms operate outside of your application, triggering even if the device is asleep\342\200\235). In the receiver, create and post a notification using NotificationManager. The notification must have:\n\nA valid channel (create one in onStartup; Android 8.0+ requires a NotificationChannel).\nSound and/or vibration if those checkboxes were enabled (use NotificationCompat.Builder and setDefaults(Notification.DEFAULT_ALL) for both by default).\nThe notification taps should either open the app or simply dismiss.\nAs the ProAndroidDev guide notes, exact alarms require the special permission and a NotificationChannel.\nPermissions & Manifest\nIn AndroidManifest.xml, declare the needed permissions:\n\nxml\nCopy\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.USE_EXACT_ALARM\" />    <!-- if targeting API 33+ -->\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS\"/>\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.VIBRATE\"/>\nSCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM/USE_EXACT_ALARM: Required on Android 12+ to allow exact-time alarms.\nPOST_NOTIFICATIONS: Required on Android 13+ for runtime notification permission.\nVIBRATE: Allows using the device vibrator (normal permission).\nAlso declare the BroadcastReceiver that will handle alarms. If you want reminders to persist after reboot, include RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED and re-schedule alarms on boot.\n\nMaterial 3 Styling and Theming\nUse Material Design 3 (Material You) components and color scheme. For example, use MaterialTheme.colorScheme for theming. In Compose, a TopAppBar could use:\n\nkotlin\nCopy\nTopAppBar(\n  colors = TopAppBarDefaults.topAppBarColors(\n    containerColor = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primaryContainer,\n    titleContentColor = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primary\n  ),\n  title = { /* \342\200\246 */ }\n)\nThis ensures your UI follows Android 12/13 dynamic color guidelines.\n\nImplementation Notes\nFor each reminder, schedule one or more alarms via AlarmManager.setExactAndAllowWhileIdle() or setAlarmClock(), so it fires at precise times even in Doze mode. Android docs emphasize using AlarmManager for exact, timely work (WorkManager is for deferrable work).\nHandle user time zone or locale changes as needed (re-schedule if the user changes clock).\nUpdate the list in real-time: when a dose time is reached (either via the alarm or if the app is open), decrement Pending, increment Done, and update the countdown timers.\nIf all doses done, change item background and replace \342\200\234Take\342\200\235 button with \342\200\234Done.\342\200\235 By default, the item background should be a light color (e.g. surfaceColor), turning light-green when completed.\nPutting it all together, your Google AI Studio prompt should specify the above UI elements, behaviors, and permissions. Emphasize a single-activity architecture with one main screen (AppBar + list + FAB) and one add-reminder screen, using Android\342\200\231s native UI components and services. Embed references to AlarmManager and Notification usage for reliable reminders.\n\nSample Manifest Snippet: <manifest ...>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM\"/>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.USE_EXACT_ALARM\"/>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS\"/>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.VIBRATE\"/>\n  <application ...>\n    <!-- Activity and receiver declarations -->\n    ...\n  </application>\n</manifest>\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    }
  }
}
